WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.960
- Okay, so just let me go
back to the main point here,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.380
which is to really thank
Ali for the amount of work

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.330
that she has put into this conference.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.920
I'm really thrilled to be here

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.142
and I am really excited about a day

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:21.059
in which I'm sure I'm gonna learn a lot

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:23.372
because as you will discover very quickly,

00:00:23.372 --> 00:00:25.620
my knowledge of the world ends

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.060
around the Industrial Revolution.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.420
So it is really thrilling to be here.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.922
So just to give you a
little bit of context here,

00:00:31.922 --> 00:00:34.230
I am going to plunge this conversation

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.150
about assemblies and lotteries

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:39.120
into a sort of a broader
historical context here,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.233
looking at what I will, sort
of like a defining moment,

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:46.770
as instances of historical
collective governance.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.750
And so here I don't really have,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.120
let me just begin with a
shout-out to my collaborators

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.746
without whom this project
would never have existed,

00:00:54.746 --> 00:00:57.330
including Nathan Schneider at
the University of Colorado,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.840
and one of our postgraduate
RAs, Riley Wong,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.120
who's done a bunch of the analysis

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.677
that I will present today,

00:01:04.677 --> 00:01:08.286
and a bunch of RAs at Kings where I work.

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:10.170
In this context, in this room,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.726
I don't have to articulate so much

00:01:12.726 --> 00:01:16.032
the sort of like motivating
question behind the project,

00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:18.474
which is, of course,
something along the lines

00:01:18.474 --> 00:01:21.570
of what Allie has already articulated.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.730
It's not so much per se, the sort of like,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.890
a collapse of of liberal democracy,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.865
although that is certainly
behind the scenes,

00:01:27.865 --> 00:01:31.020
it is also just the sheer surge in demand

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.126
in spaces for participation.

00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:34.200
Something that I, as a historian,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.690
particularly historian
across of classical Athens,

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.120
have sort of like a grown up studying,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.717
but it is certainly not something that

00:01:40.717 --> 00:01:44.217
at least until like 10
years ago, so to speak,

00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:47.130
really permeated the work of politics.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.999
And we could, Allie has
already shown some evidence

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:53.386
of the sort of like a, the
steepness of this surge,

00:01:53.386 --> 00:01:57.360
but also the variety of
instances in which we see sort

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.710
of like forms of participation,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.121
sort of like a popup in modern
politics online and offline.

00:02:03.121 --> 00:02:06.420
Things like political
parties registering citizen

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.749
permanent institutions like
redistricting commissions,

00:02:08.749 --> 00:02:11.302
and of course the climate
assemblies that have taken up

00:02:11.302 --> 00:02:16.302
so much of the sort of a newspaper,

00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:21.060
front pages in recent years.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.950
So the sort of like a motivating question

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.780
for a historian like me or someone trained

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.000
in history like me is that we sort

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.630
of like fret a lot about
these modern experiments

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.550
with participatory governance.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.130
But of course participatory
governance is not a

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.330
21st century phenomenon.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.448
It is something that is
suddenly aided by technology,

00:02:39.448 --> 00:02:44.040
but that did not begin in 1990 or 1970.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.675
And so the problem though, is
that once you start looking

00:02:46.675 --> 00:02:49.320
into collective governance
in an historical perspective,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.610
what you get is a pretty sort

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.680
of like a strange story.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.410
And the story begins with the
sort of like a suitability

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.456
for humans to live in small
bands of hunter/gatherers,

00:02:58.456 --> 00:03:01.950
where horizontal governance was
easy of course for a variety

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.950
of reasons, heterogeneous,
homogeneous preferences,

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.540
small scale and so on and so forth.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.580
And then we see sort of like a

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.090
large-scale collective
governance experiments pop up

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.386
throughout history, but in a
particular part of the world.

00:03:15.386 --> 00:03:18.090
So this is of course classical Athens

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.660
and Republican Rome, the sort of the

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.990
17th century and onward
experiences of early modern Europe.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.530
But of course collective
governance in historical

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.680
perspective is not a European phenomenon,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.390
as this story, this very
influential story seems to suggest.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.510
We have plenty of evidence of
collective governance outside

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.020
of Europe from various
disciplines, archeology,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.120
anthropology, some political science.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.880
But it is, this evidence,
is really not systematized.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.460
And as so, the result I
believe, is that we don't

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.400
really have a clear view
of other world conditions,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.370
collective governance arises, survives

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.160
or sort of like it flourishes and when

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.458
and in other world conditions it fails.

00:04:04.458 --> 00:04:07.530
So the project that I'm
gonna be describing a

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.570
little bit today, before I delve more

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.570
specifically into the question
of historical assemblies

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.690
and the use of the lot is a project

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.093
that looks at instances
of collective governance

00:04:19.093 --> 00:04:22.890
throughout history and across the world,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:27.150
which is arguably a
slightly over-ambitious way

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.000
of stating what this project is all about.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.040
And I can talk a little
bit about the limit

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.870
and the boundaries of this in the Q&amp;A,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.670
if people are interested, but the reasons

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.860
why we're doing this are twofold.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.030
First, again, if we sort of
like believe the standard story

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.423
of collective governance
and the European evidence

00:04:44.423 --> 00:04:47.580
that we have for it, I think it is fair

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.193
to say the theories that we build on,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.690
on this evidence are limited, are partial.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.120
And so what we want to
do with this evidence

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.790
that I will describe
in a moment is to sort

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.940
of like broaden our theories
of collective governance.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.520
But the theory and the source

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.410
of science is not the whole story.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.540
With a bunch of collaborators also trying

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.217
to turn this data set
into a tool that people,

00:05:12.217 --> 00:05:14.100
the communities online and offline,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.280
can actually use to build more resilient,

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.260
self-governing practices today.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.960
And of course part of the
sort of like me being here,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.668
I guess today, is that this data set

00:05:23.668 --> 00:05:26.670
among other things can be
used to take a look at,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.850
for example, particular
mechanisms such as the lotteries.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.070
So I'm gonna be doing that today.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.500
But before we get there,
I wanna spend a little bit

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.730
of time describing the database,

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.820
without which I think it'd
be hard to get the context,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.128
and give you some preliminary
findings from the two years

00:05:41.128 --> 00:05:44.850
of data collection that
we have conducted so far.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.639
I'll spend a little bit of time describing

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:50.700
what we know about the use
of the lot, which is indeed,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.710
spoiler alert, not so much.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.140
And then I'm gonna try to
give you a bit of context on

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:59.582
how historical assemblies
actually got things done,

00:05:59.582 --> 00:06:02.850
including but also beside
the use of the lot.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.000
And then Allie sort of
like provoked me a bit

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.470
for this last bit to give you
a sense of like how I use,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.342
or how I imagine this evidence,

00:06:12.342 --> 00:06:15.150
this historical evidence
might be used in the process

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.580
of actually thinking about
institution building today.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.760
So after a lot of talking
about history, we're gonna get

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.260
to the meat at the very end,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.150
so please stay awake for that.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.530
It's gonna be great.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.950
Okay, so I'm gonna be describing

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.020
for a few minutes what
the database looks like.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.880
And this is sort of gonna
include some definitions.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.090
So two years of data collection,
we've got 66 communities,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.430
not a lot guys, but
this is what we've got.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.730
The communities that enter
the data set are communities

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.160
that meet at least one criterion

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.600
or necessarily one criterion,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.660
that they have one collective
governance institution

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.730
that is an institution
within which a group

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.620
of more than one person makes decisions

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.240
on behalf of a community.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.150
The crucial criterion for

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.430
what defines a collective
governance institution,

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.700
is drawn by, from the
work of David Stasavage.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.287
This is for the sort of
the dorks in the room,

00:07:08.287 --> 00:07:11.520
that are like really into the
social science behind this.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.370
If you don't know who David
Stasavage is, it doesn't matter.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.390
The idea here is what, I
suppose that many of you

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.238
who are scholars will,
but the bottom line is,

00:07:21.238 --> 00:07:24.000
the idea is that participants in these

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.520
collective governance institutions
need to be independent

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.140
of a higher authority if it exists.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.840
And so this is the basic
criterion discriminating

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.440
between early democracy and
early autocracy in David's work,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.150
for those of you who know
what I'm talking about.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.240
So 66 communities within.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.073
Yes, please.

00:07:40.073 --> 00:07:42.510
- [Audience Member] Quick
clarifying question.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.091
What does the high authority here?

00:07:44.091 --> 00:07:46.210
What are we looking at the principle or?

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.451
- Yeah, this would be a
higher authority understood,

00:07:48.451 --> 00:07:52.410
like a king or an emperor or even like,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.020
a higher governor, right?

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.170
And so the idea here is that
if the line of command is one

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:02.040
of dependency, you are essentially
like, implementing rules

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:03.690
that come up from a higher authority

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.270
that doesn't really meet
the basic requirement.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.583
- [Audience Member] So voters would not be

00:08:07.583 --> 00:08:08.528
an example of higher authority?

00:08:08.528 --> 00:08:11.503
- No, no, no, that would not be the case.

00:08:11.503 --> 00:08:14.550
So within each community there
are a number of institutions,

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.190
some of which are institutions
for collective governance,

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.890
some are not, and so we're
looking actually at the nesting

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.940
of collective governance practices
within larger structures.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.700
And each institution has
what we call mechanisms.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.830
And the mechanisms are
really the nitty gritty

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.490
of how people got things
done within the institutions,

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:36.240
the actual rules, lotteries, elections,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.660
the various types of sort of like tools

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.040
that we then further
classified or categorized

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.950
in mechanisms that define
access to the institutions,

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:50.940
mechanisms that define
decision-making practices

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.180
and mechanism that regulate
enforcement practices.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.150
Okay, so this is just to
give you an overview of

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.650
what we've got in the dataset.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.650
We have got coverage of
all regions of the world,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:04.050
except that of course the evidence

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.867
from Europe is preponderant.

00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:10.006
I doubt that this is ever gonna go away,

00:09:10.006 --> 00:09:13.020
but this is definitely a
product of what the evidence,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.500
what kind of evidence
is easily accessible.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:17.880
But essentially by a bunch of undergrads

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.100
at King's College, London,
which is a bunch of evidence

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:20.933
from Europe,

00:09:20.933 --> 00:09:23.250
We are now changing
dramatically the way in which

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.053
we conduct the data collection,

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:27.900
which is gonna bring us in contact

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.150
with communities in
various parts of the world,

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:33.870
that are not Western
Europe and North America.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.660
And I can talk a little
bit about that second stage

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.126
in the Q&amp;A if anyone is interested.

00:09:38.126 --> 00:09:40.876
But for the time being,
what matters here is that

00:09:40.876 --> 00:09:43.950
what we're trying to do
here is actually again,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.820
shift the focus of attention beyond Europe

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.009
and to try to understand

00:09:49.009 --> 00:09:52.320
what collective governance looks like

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.420
in other places of the world.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:55.830
When we look at the communities by size,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.340
we see this again, like,
relatively broad coverage,

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.223
but a significant cluster around 10,

00:10:01.223 --> 00:10:04.276
the sort of like the size
that includes memberships

00:10:04.276 --> 00:10:07.320
between 10,000 and a hundred
thousand participants.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.320
So not large, but also not
tiny in historical perspective.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.699
And when we look at the
time we see a clustering

00:10:14.699 --> 00:10:17.130
of communities in the first millennium BC

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.720
and in the second millennium AD.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.157
This again, I doubt is an
actual empirical result

00:10:21.157 --> 00:10:24.390
of the analysis and it's
mostly driven by the kind

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.550
of evidence that we have at our disposal

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:29.640
that covers the sort of
like a usual suspects

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:33.450
from the largely sort
of European tradition.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.329
So we will see if by looking
at other non-European cases,

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:39.663
the timeline shifts with the evidence.

00:10:41.182 --> 00:10:43.170
I just wanted to give you a sense of like,

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:44.780
what these mechanisms look like.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.830
So of course this matters to you

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:51.830
because it includes the
lotteries as a mechanism,

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.560
I will talk about this
in a moment, of access

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.830
as a fundamental, one of the
primary perhaps mechanism

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.269
for accessing in collective
governance institutions.

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:04.752
But as you can see, there are
a number of other mechanisms

00:11:04.752 --> 00:11:08.130
that come up in this, in this data set

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:13.010
that suggest variation
across selection mechanisms.

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.548
We see, when we look at
decision-making mechanisms,

00:11:15.548 --> 00:11:18.390
again, a preponderance of usual suspects,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.990
such as councils and assemblies.

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:24.360
But we also see sort of
like the growing importance

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.720
of things like deliberation,
consensus and representation.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.690
And we look at enforcement
again, we see courts

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.370
and tribunals as the primary bodies

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.530
for enforcement procedures, but also

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.170
some interesting evidence
on the use of scrutinies

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:40.230
and practices like ostracism
and judicial review.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.850
So this is just to give you a sense

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:44.820
of what is in this data set.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.100
So as I said, we have spent a couple

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.744
of years collecting evidence.

00:11:48.744 --> 00:11:51.210
Again, a bunch of this
evidence is from Europe.

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:55.050
We're trying to push that sort
of like, push against that.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:56.850
But we are not quite there yet.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:58.800
We have spent a good chunk of our summer,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.780
this past summer looking at exactly

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.115
what this database is telling us

00:12:02.115 --> 00:12:03.387
about collective governance.

00:12:03.387 --> 00:12:06.715
And so I'm just gonna be
mentioning three results,

00:12:06.715 --> 00:12:11.715
which I think are perhaps
like promising enough

00:12:11.925 --> 00:12:15.870
that I still think that this
work should be continued,

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:16.950
I should continue to do this.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:18.735
But maybe this is something
that you can advise me

00:12:18.735 --> 00:12:22.116
on towards the end of this presentation,

00:12:22.116 --> 00:12:25.427
if you think that there's no there there.

00:12:25.427 --> 00:12:30.270
So the primary finding
that seems of relevance is

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:34.200
that the mechanisms that
we see operating within

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:38.070
collective governance
communities that are in Europe

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.680
are not the same as the
mechanisms that we see

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.810
otherwise in other places over the world.

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:43.855
So that is quite interesting.

00:12:43.855 --> 00:12:45.510
This is just to give you a sense of

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:47.130
what this looks like in the correlations,

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:49.445
that is a cluster of
mechanisms that we associate

00:12:49.445 --> 00:12:53.834
with collective governance
today in the United States.

00:12:53.834 --> 00:12:56.790
We tend to think about collective
governance as something

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:00.750
that has assemblies, as
we've heard today, councils,

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:04.140
and things like lotteries,
term limits, rotation.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:07.380
These mechanisms are very
familiar to the Western tradition

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:08.850
of collective governance.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.430
But it turns out that when
we look outside of Europe,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:16.430
these mechanisms cease to
be present just simply.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.110
And when we see correlations

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:21.420
between collective
governance outside of Europe

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.010
and particular mechanisms,

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.293
we see something else
popping up, essentially.

00:13:25.293 --> 00:13:27.420
These include informal sanctions,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.932
consultation procedures,
popularity meritocracy

00:13:30.932 --> 00:13:34.713
and local councils and blood
relations and meritocracy.

00:13:36.537 --> 00:13:39.000
And again, like, there is not
just variation between Europe

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.410
and other parts of the world, but also

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:45.410
within non-European
sort of like locations,

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:46.950
we see variation as well.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:50.369
And so this is really for us,
a sort of like a indication

00:13:50.369 --> 00:13:53.280
that we should perhaps, continue
with the data collection

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.260
in these other places
and try to figure out

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:58.200
what kind of picture we get
of collective governance

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.960
when we take a more
global perspective on it.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.677
A couple of more, perhaps less,
sort of like a interesting

00:14:03.677 --> 00:14:07.410
and a little bit boring findings,
but please bear with me.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.020
There seems to be an
inverted-U shaped relationship

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.550
between size and duration.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.710
This is quite interesting because it seems

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.970
to push against the idea

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.785
that collective governance
collapses at scale.

00:14:17.785 --> 00:14:21.840
It seems what we find
in the dataset so far is

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:24.480
that there is a sort of like a sweet spot,

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.450
where collective governance
communities seem to last longer.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.240
Again, lasting longer is not
necessarily a sign of success,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.330
this is problematic, but this
is what the data says so far.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:37.230
And so we sort of like,
this seems to be like

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:38.610
some sort of like illustrative evidence

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.280
that suggests that there is
something more interesting

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.620
about scale and collective governance than

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.260
what we tend to imagine,
which is essentially

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.270
that as scale increases the probability

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:50.020
of collective governance decreases.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:54.420
The third finding that
I want to talk about is,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:56.370
again, variation by size.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:59.280
So this is a similar perhaps, result,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.610
and it again suggests that
there is something going on

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:04.980
at the level of different communities

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.950
of different sizes seem to be operating

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.120
through different mechanisms.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.700
So again, this is perhaps
something to keep in mind

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:15.390
as we think about building
collective governance spaces.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.790
It seems from the evidence that we've got

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:19.680
that different mechanisms like,

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.260
a mechanism might not apply equally

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:23.880
to communities of various sizes.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:27.474
And here when we look
at sort of mechanisms

00:15:27.474 --> 00:15:32.040
with European cases, we see
this clustering of a series

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:34.020
of mechanisms at a particular,

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.480
sort of this middle range sizes.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:41.160
When we take Europe out, we see
more variation across sizes.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.860
And again, this is in
part, the problem here is

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.540
that we don't have a lot of evidence,

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.610
as I mentioned about non-European cases.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.460
We certainly don't have
as robust evidence about

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.820
non-European cases, but these
results are sort of like,

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.490
illustrative of something that is going on

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.860
in sort of like different
parts of the world.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:00.360
They might tell us something new

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.220
about collective governance.

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:03.960
Let me just stop for a second

