So I'm gonna actually
go quickly through this.

00:28:10.772 --> 00:28:13.800
And this is something that I spend a lot

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:15.660
of time on, which is like, okay,

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:16.920
let's say that I do these things because

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:18.480
I'm a classical historian by training,

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:20.760
which is only part of
the reason, arguably.

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:24.420
And I study historical collective
governance because again,

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:27.450
this is what someone
trained as a historian does.

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:29.325
I actually think that
there's a little bit more

00:28:29.325 --> 00:28:32.010
to it than that, and I
think that this evidence is

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:34.140
actually fundamental to understand how

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:36.330
to build institutions today, particularly

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:39.030
at a time when sort of like
Western liberalism seems

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.970
to be at the point of like descendance

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.830
as opposed to like ascendance.

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.920
But the question of like how
to actually use this evidence

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:50.520
to build institutions today
is to me, not that obvious.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.290
And so some of us have
been spending a little bit

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:55.238
of time trying to write about
it and try to think about it.

00:28:55.238 --> 00:28:57.210
My colleague Nathan Schneider,

00:28:57.210 --> 00:29:00.060
and David Stasavage at NYU,
whom I mentioned earlier,

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:02.820
wrote this like very nice
short piece in NOEMA,

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:06.120
where they suggest how
lessons from early democracies

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:11.120
or historical collective
governance communities can be used

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:14.490
to think about platform regulation.

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:17.640
And a couple of years ago I
wrote a sort of provocative,

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:22.110
perhaps, piece in "Wired"
that was sort of like meant

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:25.410
to use the example of the Athenian council

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.020
to think about algorithmic accountability.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.500
So I tend to never mention
that piece to anyone,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.870
but Allie knows about it and
she asked me to talk about it.

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:35.940
So that's, it's her fault essentially.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:38.040
- [Allie] What did you say in the piece?

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:39.935
- Oh look, let me tell you all about it.

00:29:39.935 --> 00:29:43.980
So this again, the sort of
like the provocation came

00:29:43.980 --> 00:29:47.130
from my involvement in an Open AI report

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:49.050
that came out in 2020, which for questions

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:50.100
about algorithm accountability,

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:52.317
this is equivalent to ancient history.

00:29:52.317 --> 00:29:54.660
The report was on trustworthy AI

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:56.250
and the report essentially sort of like,

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:59.520
summarized the question of
like how to build institutions

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:01.710
for algorithm accountability,

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:03.930
that actually included some
sort of like accountability

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:07.020
to the companies, with the words like,

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:08.910
we should create external auditing bodies.

00:30:08.910 --> 00:30:11.880
But of course the report
never sort of like mentioned

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.100
what those auditing
bodies ought to look like.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.180
And so the piece was meant

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:17.880
to sort of like provoke
people into thinking

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:20.310
about how to use historical evidence

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:23.940
for thinking about this
kind of institution building

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:26.250
in ungoverned spaces, perhaps,

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.950
still ungoverned, certainly in the U.S.

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:31.530
So again, the premises of
the piece were the sort

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:33.750
of like, just provoke
readers into thinking

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:36.391
about the fundamental
clash between the use

00:30:36.391 --> 00:30:38.550
of algorithms in society and thinking

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:40.320
about democracies and assemblies.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:44.550
In one case you get sort
of an optimization function

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:48.060
that tells you this is
the best possible outcome,

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.250
but of course this is not something

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.380
that will surprise many
of you in the room.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:53.969
This is not how politics works.

00:30:53.969 --> 00:30:57.600
And in fact, based on what
might we might describe

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:59.700
as the original lesson
of democracy, these types

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:03.540
of societal trade-offs are
best addressed by listening

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:06.720
to varied voices, by many voices.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:09.383
And so the piece was sort of like, again,

00:31:09.383 --> 00:31:11.970
trying to look at empirical evidence

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:15.360
of how large-scale citizen governance

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:18.360
on tough questions might occur.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.130
And so the provocation was

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:24.630
to model a idealized institution

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.460
for algorithmic accountability on the

00:31:26.460 --> 00:31:27.935
Athenian council of 500.

00:31:27.935 --> 00:31:30.246
And here the devil's
in the details, right?

00:31:30.246 --> 00:31:33.390
I don't wanna sort of like
bore you too much with this,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:37.078
but the idea here is that
the Boule featured very few,

00:31:37.078 --> 00:31:40.290
very interesting rules
that in a sort of like,

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:44.220
nice little package ensured
broad citizen participation

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:45.600
and representation.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.970
So let me address some of
the questions that Allie got

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:49.710
because the Athenians
seem to have sort of like,

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.810
a thought about
stratification in a slightly

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:54.300
different way, which is
essentially over time.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.540
And so they used a lot to select people

00:31:57.540 --> 00:32:00.720
across relevant cleavages,
can talk about that again,

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:01.740
until your eyes pop out.

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:03.780
But let me not bore you too much.

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.040
They had an annual mandate.

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:06.810
So you're in and then you're out.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.678
Demand cannot be iterated.

00:32:08.678 --> 00:32:11.430
It cannot be iterated consecutively

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:13.260
and in general, you
cannot iterate the mandate

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:14.700
for more than two years.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:15.533
The result of this,

00:32:15.533 --> 00:32:17.370
some classical historians
have done the numbers,

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:20.230
is that a huge chunk of the Athenian part

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:23.850
of the population that was eligible

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:26.430
for participation in
politics, a huge chunk of

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:29.490
that population actually,
at one point in their lives,

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:31.500
participated in this institution.

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:34.230
And that had some really interesting sort

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:36.654
of like a knock-on effect.

00:32:36.654 --> 00:32:41.654
The knock-on effects are
that these rules enable

00:32:41.666 --> 00:32:46.200
this particular institution
to avoid some of the pitfalls

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:48.840
that we normally associate
with collective governance,

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:51.570
such as the possibility of lead capture,

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:54.480
but also the idea that a
collective governance is

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:57.570
just a bunch of ignorant
people, in the Athenian case,

00:32:57.570 --> 00:32:59.850
sitting on a hill making
decisions about things

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:00.780
that they don't know anything

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:02.490
and therefore that's
why they need experts.

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:06.150
And the Athenians seem to
have sort of like overcome

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:09.060
this problem through the system of rules

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:12.150
that promote knowledge
aggregation within the institution

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:14.880
at a given time and learning over time.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:18.517
So again, I don't wanna
sort of like spend more

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:22.320
than I have really
spent, in terms of time,

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.330
sort of like trying to
convince you that there is

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:26.280
something valuable thinking about

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.032
these historical institutions

00:33:28.032 --> 00:33:30.510
for institution building today.

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:34.290
But I hope to have given you a
sense that it is in fact sort

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:35.970
of like, perhaps about time,

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:38.940
to think about experiments
in collective governance

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.616
that are both past and global,

00:33:41.616 --> 00:33:44.130
to think about systems
of collective governance

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:47.070
that go beyond this sort
of like a real interesting,

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.097
but also peculiar in my perspective,

00:33:49.097 --> 00:33:51.540
sort of like a focus on the lot,

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:54.909
and also to think very,
very clearly in sort

00:33:54.909 --> 00:33:59.400
of like a clear eyed-ly,
perhaps, about application

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:02.520
of this evidence to modern
institution building.

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:05.193
I'm gonna shut up. Thank
you for your consideration.

00:34:06.258 --> 00:34:09.508
(audience applauding)

00:34:10.957 --> 00:34:15.153
Some of you are still
awake, I think, Allie.

00:34:21.990 --> 00:34:25.323
- [Allie] Hi, I'm Allie Cerone,
you've heard me talk a lot.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.350
So one thing I think that's interesting

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:31.770
about this research is one scale.

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.840
So a lot of the people
in this room are trying

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:36.360
to figure out can we scale-
- Right.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:38.610
- [Allie] Not just governance,
but different type of scale,

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:41.580
but to what extent, like, can
deliberative groups be bigger?

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:44.400
Can we have multiple of them running?

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:47.580
Can we start doing thousands
of people in assemblies?

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.820
So what does your research
teach us about scale?

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:53.340
And this kind of goes
back to Costa's question.

00:34:53.340 --> 00:34:55.410
It's interesting because
we see historically,

00:34:55.410 --> 00:34:58.530
a lot of the cool stuff with
lotteries happened in Europe-

00:34:58.530 --> 00:34:59.363
- Right.
- [Allie] But these were

00:34:59.363 --> 00:35:01.420
also the first democracies, they were also

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:03.450
easier to govern, they were richer.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:05.130
There's a lot of like other correlates

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:06.360
that might kind explain this,

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:08.629
but it is the case that
even today, so in the U.S.,

00:35:08.629 --> 00:35:10.620
we have a lot of people in this room

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:14.280
that are doing U.S.-based activities,

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:16.950
but Europe is by far
surpassing us in terms

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:19.560
of the experimentation, the novelty,

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:21.990
and like having this be routine.

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:24.000
So people know about citizens
assemblies in Europe,

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:26.280
the average American citizen
I would argue does not.

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:29.850
And so is there, is
there a European magic?

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:31.208
Is this just kind of path dependence,

00:35:31.208 --> 00:35:33.420
is there something, do you think,

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:36.003
that the European way can be exported?

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:38.460
- Yeah, so about scale,
I think that this is,

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:41.340
in fact, one of the biggest
takeaway from this research is

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:43.650
to push against the idea
that there is something

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:47.100
about a small scale that
favors collective governance

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:49.680
and something about large
scale that makes it impossible.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.650
And again, I think that the
devil's in the details here.

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.870
I'm not gonna say that
that fundamental assumption

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:57.060
about the sort of like what makes

00:35:57.060 --> 00:36:00.180
collective governance
flourish is falsified

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:01.200
by what I'm seeing.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:02.490
What I'm seeing is in fact

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:05.130
that collective governance
institutions occur at any scale

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:08.550
in a polity from very,
very small to pretty large.

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:12.570
And what seems to be
particularly relevant is the ways

00:36:12.570 --> 00:36:14.757
in which they are nested
within other institutions.

00:36:14.757 --> 00:36:17.070
And so I think that this
is in fact something

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:19.050
that the database can really give us

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.209
because we are not gonna have a congress

00:36:21.209 --> 00:36:24.477
of lottery-elected participants.

00:36:24.477 --> 00:36:27.180
And so I think that the
question for us is not so much

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:28.590
to think about substitution,

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:30.510
but to think about like, ways in which

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:33.690
we can integrate systems
of collective governance

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:36.150
within other hierarchical representative

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:37.710
and various types of institutions.

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:40.878
So scale to me is a red herring, per se.

00:36:40.878 --> 00:36:44.910
The real question is
about like, articulation

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:47.490
of various types of
institutions, with potentially

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:49.770
very different mechanisms of selection,

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:51.540
of decision making,
and so on and so forth.

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:55.110
And again, I don't think that
we're gonna get much past

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:58.950
that even almost already ideal version

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:00.240
of doing politics.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:02.670
I think that participation
has a role to play,

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:04.080
but not everywhere, perhaps.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:05.580
And this is again,
like, the social science

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:07.560
that we need is really
about these questions.

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:10.020
So that's about scale.

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:11.857
On Europe, I think that Europe has,

00:37:11.857 --> 00:37:13.620
that's interesting, I don't know.

00:37:13.620 --> 00:37:15.330
But the answer to your
question is I don't know,

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:20.160
but I think that Europe
has a long-term tradition

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:23.310
of types and forms of
collective governance.

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:26.353
It also has of course,
for a variety of reasons,

00:37:26.353 --> 00:37:28.050
political fragmentation is something

00:37:28.050 --> 00:37:31.005
that political scientists love
to think about all the time.

00:37:31.005 --> 00:37:35.160
A sort of like a more like, ease

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:38.845
with less hierarchical
forms of governance.

00:37:38.845 --> 00:37:41.493
I don't think that the United
States is in a particularly

00:37:41.493 --> 00:37:44.460
sort of like a different position.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:45.870
And I think that there's
actually quite a bit

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:47.250
of experimentation in the U.S.

00:37:47.250 --> 00:37:49.050
It's just the U.S. is so much bigger.

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:51.663
And I think that it's
also probably true that,

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:53.880
I wonder if that is correct,

00:37:53.880 --> 00:37:55.501
like what are the expectations

00:37:55.501 --> 00:37:57.030
of the average European citizen

00:37:57.030 --> 00:37:58.680
versus the average American citizen?

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:00.300
I think that they change
also dramatically,

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:02.056
so I'm not quite sure that it is true

